WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.935
&gt;&gt; So thank you for
inviting me today,

00:00:06.936 --> 00:00:09.650
I'm really excited to be here.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.120
The title of my presentation
probably you should say

00:00:13.121 --> 00:00:16.150
that I'm from an
Information Science School.

00:00:16.151 --> 00:00:18.165
I'm a PhD candidate,

00:00:18.166 --> 00:00:20.445
I'm graduating in June.

00:00:20.446 --> 00:00:23.110
Okay. So the title of
my presentation is

00:00:23.111 --> 00:00:26.030
the Making of Fungible
Open Data in Biomedicine,

00:00:26.031 --> 00:00:28.300
and most of my presentation will

00:00:28.301 --> 00:00:30.520
be dedicated to explain you

00:00:30.521 --> 00:00:32.460
the socio-technical but also

00:00:32.461 --> 00:00:36.340
epistemic societal
challenges involved

00:00:36.341 --> 00:00:39.240
in reusing genomic data

00:00:39.241 --> 00:00:42.345
in Biomedicine,
Research Genomic Data.

00:00:42.346 --> 00:00:44.625
But before going into that,

00:00:44.626 --> 00:00:47.150
I also want to give
you an overview,

00:00:47.151 --> 00:00:49.790
a short overview of
my research values

00:00:49.791 --> 00:00:52.545
and interests of my domain,

00:00:52.546 --> 00:00:55.280
influences and
methods that informed

00:00:55.281 --> 00:00:59.120
my research during my
years as a PhD student and

00:00:59.121 --> 00:01:01.220
then short overview of

00:01:01.221 --> 00:01:03.170
my collaborative projects because

00:01:03.171 --> 00:01:05.960
I've done quite a lot
in collaboration with

00:01:05.961 --> 00:01:08.300
other departments so I want to

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.670
point out these projects and

00:01:10.671 --> 00:01:13.370
then we'll give you a lot of

00:01:13.371 --> 00:01:15.530
details probably more than

00:01:15.531 --> 00:01:18.900
what you want to hear about
my dissertation project.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:24.645
Since this is a job talk,

00:01:24.646 --> 00:01:26.960
I figured that I wanted you to

00:01:26.961 --> 00:01:29.150
know who I am as a researcher,

00:01:29.151 --> 00:01:30.980
what I care about
and why I want to

00:01:30.981 --> 00:01:32.930
be a researcher in
the first place because

00:01:32.931 --> 00:01:35.045
with all the things
that I could do

00:01:35.046 --> 00:01:37.955
right now especially working
with data and people,

00:01:37.956 --> 00:01:40.070
why do I want to be
a researcher, right?

00:01:40.071 --> 00:01:47.720
My main interest is
really in epistemology.

00:01:47.721 --> 00:01:49.190
Since I have memories,

00:01:49.191 --> 00:01:51.950
I have been really interested
in understanding how

00:01:51.951 --> 00:01:55.580
people make sense of
the word in different ways,

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:58.580
what kind of means they use
to make sense of the word,

00:01:58.581 --> 00:02:01.195
and why do we have all these
different perspectives,

00:02:01.196 --> 00:02:03.670
and how we end up with
different perspectives.

00:02:03.671 --> 00:02:07.430
So this is what I always
been interested into and

00:02:07.431 --> 00:02:08.840
that's also why I probably have

00:02:08.841 --> 00:02:12.065
a master degree in journalism,

00:02:12.066 --> 00:02:14.875
so it's curiosity driven.

00:02:14.876 --> 00:02:18.490
But then over
the last seven years,

00:02:18.491 --> 00:02:20.470
I really became interested in

00:02:20.471 --> 00:02:21.820
data epistemologies more

00:02:21.821 --> 00:02:24.850
specifically and
code epistemologies,

00:02:24.851 --> 00:02:28.690
and everything started
seven years ago

00:02:28.691 --> 00:02:29.710
when I was working as

00:02:29.711 --> 00:02:31.960
a journalist and also I was

00:02:31.961 --> 00:02:34.695
finishing my master
in media studies.

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:37.085
And during that time,

00:02:37.086 --> 00:02:42.355
I witnessed the widespread
sudden diffusion

00:02:42.356 --> 00:02:44.455
of data and adoption of

00:02:44.456 --> 00:02:48.030
data-driven techniques
by Italian journalists.

00:02:48.031 --> 00:02:50.595
It was a new thing back then,

00:02:50.596 --> 00:02:54.765
and this was because the Italian
government back then,

00:02:54.766 --> 00:02:58.500
it was inspired by the
Obama's Open Data Initiative,

00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:02.010
started to release all these
statistics and data and

00:03:02.011 --> 00:03:04.290
demographic and
transportation data

00:03:04.291 --> 00:03:05.790
about Italian citizens.

00:03:05.791 --> 00:03:07.830
So as journalists we were very

00:03:07.831 --> 00:03:10.710
enthusiastic of how these
[inaudible] was back in

00:03:10.711 --> 00:03:13.380
my master thesis which
was supposed to be on

00:03:13.381 --> 00:03:14.700
these topics of how to use

00:03:14.701 --> 00:03:17.055
the statistics for storytelling.

00:03:17.056 --> 00:03:21.150
And myself I wrote some
articles using APIs from

00:03:21.151 --> 00:03:25.570
Twitter and Facebook and
talking about how then,

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:28.610
already how the network
back then was

00:03:28.611 --> 00:03:32.150
talking about specific and
understanding social issues,

00:03:32.151 --> 00:03:34.745
but then at some point I stopped.

00:03:34.746 --> 00:03:37.265
And I stopped because I realized

00:03:37.266 --> 00:03:39.815
that we really had no
idea what we were doing.

00:03:39.816 --> 00:03:42.620
So as journalists, we had

00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:45.535
no training in social
science research methods.

00:03:45.536 --> 00:03:49.835
We had no conception of issues
of validity, reliability,

00:03:49.836 --> 00:03:52.250
generalizations,
bias in the data,

00:03:52.251 --> 00:03:54.650
we didn't know anything
about these things,

00:03:54.651 --> 00:03:57.350
and I saw that some of us

00:03:57.351 --> 00:04:00.354
we were using
statistics to become

00:04:00.355 --> 00:04:07.620
big claims that were not always
back with good datasets.

00:04:07.621 --> 00:04:10.410
And then especially I was
particularly bothered

00:04:10.411 --> 00:04:13.575
while by one event.

00:04:13.576 --> 00:04:15.840
So the Italian government
was trying to pass

00:04:15.841 --> 00:04:18.270
this law on sexual harassment,

00:04:18.271 --> 00:04:21.850
on the phenomenon of
sexual harassment in Italy,

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.260
and some of the
journalists they use

00:04:25.261 --> 00:04:28.230
some public statistics to
undermine these phenomenon

00:04:28.231 --> 00:04:31.410
and showing that it wasn't
such a big thing as the people

00:04:31.411 --> 00:04:33.420
were pushing for this law

00:04:33.421 --> 00:04:35.775
to pass while saying
that it was them.

00:04:35.776 --> 00:04:38.990
So at that point I decided to,

00:04:38.991 --> 00:04:41.500
instead of writing
my dissertation about how

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.930
to use statistics
for storytelling,

00:04:43.931 --> 00:04:47.440
more about the limits
and how the statistics.

00:04:47.441 --> 00:04:52.259
So I became more interested
in writing about statistics

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.225
then using the type of

00:04:54.226 --> 00:04:57.085
this data analysis myself
for my own storytelling.

00:04:57.086 --> 00:04:59.310
If that makes sense
to you guys I think.

00:04:59.311 --> 00:05:02.155
Okay, and that's
also when I realized

00:05:02.156 --> 00:05:05.620
that there's none of which
served in 2011 wherever.

00:05:05.621 --> 00:05:09.910
That in our data economy
and Information Society,

00:05:09.911 --> 00:05:11.860
there are really
three emerging groups of

00:05:11.861 --> 00:05:14.500
people: the data creators;

00:05:14.501 --> 00:05:16.165
those who consciously or

00:05:16.166 --> 00:05:19.910
unconsciously could
produce all the data,

00:05:19.911 --> 00:05:23.160
unconsciously like in
social media I guess.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:27.540
Those who have the expertise
to analyze the data,

00:05:27.541 --> 00:05:31.090
and those who have the means
to access and collect data.

00:05:31.091 --> 00:05:34.160
And I realized that
as a researcher,

00:05:34.161 --> 00:05:36.095
I was interested in
all these topics

00:05:36.096 --> 00:05:37.220
and you will see it's

00:05:37.221 --> 00:05:40.310
how issues of epistemology,

00:05:40.311 --> 00:05:43.914
data production, but also
a lot data governance

00:05:43.915 --> 00:05:48.360
come up in my research across
all different projects.

00:05:48.361 --> 00:05:52.990
So I was never really able
to focus just on one side,

00:05:52.991 --> 00:05:57.560
so it's stuff that
I'm interested into.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.610
Yeah, I guess I should
also point out that

00:06:00.611 --> 00:06:03.880
today I'm mainly
a qualitative researcher even

00:06:03.881 --> 00:06:06.610
though I have some training
in programming and

00:06:06.611 --> 00:06:10.725
statistics and other types
of research methods,

00:06:10.726 --> 00:06:12.240
but I'm mainly an ethnographer.

00:06:12.241 --> 00:06:14.125
Well, we'll say
something more about it.

00:06:14.126 --> 00:06:15.940
So influence is a method.

00:06:15.941 --> 00:06:18.730
I have a PhD into
disciplinary background,

00:06:18.731 --> 00:06:20.830
so I studied
communication and then

00:06:20.831 --> 00:06:22.855
have this Master
in media studies,

00:06:22.856 --> 00:06:27.055
but during my period
as a PhD student,

00:06:27.056 --> 00:06:28.510
I think there are

00:06:28.511 --> 00:06:33.740
three main areas that really
impacted me as a researcher.

00:06:33.741 --> 00:06:36.700
Of course Information Science
and classification theory,

00:06:36.701 --> 00:06:38.350
maybe some of you can recognize

00:06:38.351 --> 00:06:41.705
the cover of the book from
'Bulkier and Star' and

00:06:41.706 --> 00:06:44.830
my own adviser was still
big influence me a lot to

00:06:44.831 --> 00:06:47.320
Christine Borgman
who does a lot of

00:06:47.321 --> 00:06:50.440
studies in scientific
collaborations,

00:06:50.441 --> 00:06:55.180
but also social studies of
data and algorithms especially

00:06:55.181 --> 00:06:57.310
critical data studies
that is probably also

00:06:57.311 --> 00:06:59.900
feel that some of you
are familiar with.

00:06:59.901 --> 00:07:05.640
The other thing is
that basically,

00:07:05.641 --> 00:07:08.880
I like to say that I have a
minor in history of science.

00:07:08.881 --> 00:07:10.955
History of science really,

00:07:10.956 --> 00:07:13.120
a minor as a PhD student
even though it

00:07:13.121 --> 00:07:16.690
doesn't exist but, yeah.

00:07:16.691 --> 00:07:19.675
So history of science for me
was really really important.

00:07:19.676 --> 00:07:22.210
It fuel my work so much.

00:07:22.211 --> 00:07:25.030
At UCLA we have
a pretty strong department

00:07:25.031 --> 00:07:26.190
