00:07:26.191 --> 00:07:28.800
people like Thad Potter,

00:07:28.801 --> 00:07:30.615
amazing people working there.

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:34.440
And because my work is about how

00:07:34.441 --> 00:07:38.700
scientists use biology databases,

00:07:38.701 --> 00:07:40.530
for me it was impossible to

00:07:40.531 --> 00:07:42.075
make sense of these phenomenon

00:07:42.076 --> 00:07:45.420
without looking at how
it evolved through time.

00:07:45.421 --> 00:07:46.710
It was like nearly impossible,

00:07:46.711 --> 00:07:48.180
even though it was
not required to

00:07:48.181 --> 00:07:49.830
take more a historical approach,

00:07:49.831 --> 00:07:52.080
but this was really
important for me.

00:07:52.081 --> 00:07:55.020
And the second thing
is that somehow it

00:07:55.021 --> 00:07:56.910
happened that a lot of my work is

00:07:56.911 --> 00:07:58.830
related to issues of race,

00:07:58.831 --> 00:08:00.480
which was not something that want

00:08:00.481 --> 00:08:02.430
to necessarily write about but

00:08:02.431 --> 00:08:07.690
then it just keep
coming up everywhere.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:12.895
I also specialize in
the history of human variation,

00:08:12.896 --> 00:08:14.890
and I'm particularly
interested in

00:08:14.891 --> 00:08:17.720
how the biomedical field,

00:08:17.721 --> 00:08:19.975
think about the issues of race

00:08:19.976 --> 00:08:22.510
and issues of
biological determinism,

00:08:22.511 --> 00:08:24.835
I think and then everything
will make sense.

00:08:24.836 --> 00:08:27.055
Eventually, hopefully you will

00:08:27.056 --> 00:08:29.800
see that I have
