00:14:09.786 --> 00:14:11.490
is, so they download

00:14:11.491 --> 00:14:15.615
the raw data from
the companies' websites.

00:14:15.616 --> 00:14:17.970
Right because there are
these companies like 23andMe,

00:14:17.971 --> 00:14:21.420
that they run ancestry testing

00:14:21.421 --> 00:14:24.600
for you and then you can
download your raw data

00:14:24.601 --> 00:14:26.610
and you can upload
the raw data on

00:14:26.611 --> 00:14:29.070
third-party calculators and they

00:14:29.071 --> 00:14:30.570
recalculate the data
and they give

00:14:30.571 --> 00:14:32.180
you basically a different result,

00:14:32.181 --> 00:14:33.360
because it's also based on

00:14:33.361 --> 00:14:35.100
the kind of reference
populations that they

00:14:35.101 --> 00:14:38.980
have their own samples and so on.

00:14:38.981 --> 00:14:40.420
And apparently, it turns out

00:14:40.421 --> 00:14:42.280
that these alternative
calculators,

00:14:42.281 --> 00:14:44.970
they retrieve whiter results
for some reason,

00:14:44.971 --> 00:14:46.290
that it's really difficult

00:14:46.291 --> 00:14:48.975
to download because
they are black-boxed.

00:14:48.976 --> 00:14:51.945
Anyway, so this was
my secondary project.

00:14:51.946 --> 00:14:53.670
Okay, so this was kind of

00:14:53.671 --> 00:14:57.670
an overview of
my collaborative projects.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:04.170
But, the main lab
I've been working

00:15:04.171 --> 00:15:08.265
on is the Center for
Knowledge Infrastructures.

00:15:08.266 --> 00:15:12.060
I joined the Center right after I

00:15:12.061 --> 00:15:15.660
started my PhD program
and so from day

00:15:15.661 --> 00:15:19.440
one I was a research
assistant with this lab and I

00:15:19.441 --> 00:15:21.210
started collecting data at

00:15:21.211 --> 00:15:24.110
my research community
really early.

00:15:24.111 --> 00:15:26.610
And I joined this grant that

00:15:26.611 --> 00:15:29.575
basically funded
my whole PhD life.

00:15:29.576 --> 00:15:33.055
Well, not this year, but
my first three years.

00:15:33.056 --> 00:15:34.960
The grant is called,

00:15:34.961 --> 00:15:37.630
If Data Sharing is the Answer,

00:15:37.631 --> 00:15:38.995
What is the Question?

00:15:38.996 --> 00:15:41.995
And it broadly investigates

00:15:41.996 --> 00:15:44.935
this current trend in

00:15:44.936 --> 00:15:46.450
science policy that maybe

00:15:46.451 --> 00:15:48.400
you're familiar with, maybe not,

00:15:48.401 --> 00:15:51.300
which is to make
available to the public,

00:15:51.301 --> 00:15:53.670
to the whole
international community,

00:15:53.671 --> 00:15:55.865
and also to the industry,

00:15:55.866 --> 00:15:58.600
as much as possible,
research data.

00:15:58.601 --> 00:16:01.135
Okay. In our lab,

00:16:01.136 --> 00:16:03.850
we are like six researchers.

00:16:03.851 --> 00:16:08.045
Each of us, we focus on
a different discipline.

00:16:08.046 --> 00:16:11.940
So, I have a colleague who is
working on astronomy data,

00:16:11.941 --> 00:16:15.390
a colleague who is working
on Earth science, and so on.

00:16:15.391 --> 00:16:17.490
So the idea is that eventually,

00:16:17.491 --> 00:16:20.225
at the end of the project
to come together and make

00:16:20.226 --> 00:16:22.160
comparisons between
how these issues

00:16:22.161 --> 00:16:23.870
are different in
different domains.

00:16:23.871 --> 00:16:28.760
So, my specialization
is genomic medicine,

00:16:28.761 --> 00:16:32.235
and I will tell you more
about my specific case study.

00:16:32.236 --> 00:16:36.080
But before presenting
my research questions

00:16:36.081 --> 00:16:39.635
I thought that I should
give you some background,

00:16:39.636 --> 00:16:42.980
because this is
a less intuitive program

00:16:42.981 --> 00:16:45.390
compared to other
things I wrote about.

00:16:45.391 --> 00:16:47.860
You really have to
know what is going on.

00:16:47.861 --> 00:16:49.725
Okay. So, first of all,

00:16:49.726 --> 00:16:53.290
I am writing and I'm talking
about research data.

00:16:53.291 --> 00:16:55.710
So these are not
clinical trial data.

00:16:55.711 --> 00:16:57.630
These are research data that are

00:16:57.631 --> 00:17:00.840
collected to answer specific
research questions.

00:17:00.841 --> 00:17:04.860
And I'm saying this

00:17:04.861 --> 00:17:09.660
because I was at Hicks
last week and someone asked me,

00:17:09.661 --> 00:17:11.190
"What's the difference
between research data

00:17:11.191 --> 00:17:12.255
and social media data?"

00:17:12.256 --> 00:17:13.380
There is no difference. I don't

00:17:13.381 --> 00:17:15.150
understand why you
want to focus on

00:17:15.151 --> 00:17:18.060
research data as opposed
to social media data.

00:17:18.061 --> 00:17:21.490
I was like, 'I think I'm very
interested in research data

00:17:21.491 --> 00:17:23.620
because research data are

00:17:23.621 --> 00:17:26.079
used for scientific
knowledge production,

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:27.730
which is a specific type of

00:17:27.731 --> 00:17:29.290
knowledge production that comes

00:17:29.291 --> 00:17:30.820
with specific expectations.'

00:17:30.821 --> 00:17:33.550
Right? So scientific
knowledge production is,

00:17:33.551 --> 00:17:34.750
first of all, cumulative.

00:17:34.751 --> 00:17:37.465
Right? We stand on
the shoulders of the giants.

00:17:37.466 --> 00:17:40.840
So it builds upon itself and then

00:17:40.841 --> 00:17:42.460
it's peer reviewed and it's

00:17:42.461 --> 00:17:44.305
validated in a collaborative way.

00:17:44.306 --> 00:17:47.815
Right? So this is
the main reason.

00:17:47.816 --> 00:17:50.080
Now, one of the main
differences between

00:17:50.081 --> 00:17:52.300
research data and
other types of data,

00:17:52.301 --> 00:17:54.270
then, it's publicly funded,

00:17:54.271 --> 00:17:58.070
so at least the type of
data that I look at,

00:17:58.071 --> 00:18:00.620
that's another characteristic
of publicly funded,

00:18:00.621 --> 00:18:01.745
so it's an investment.

00:18:01.746 --> 00:18:05.140
So it's a resource and
basically also a commodity.

00:18:05.141 --> 00:18:09.140
And then I read this difference
in a paper and I found,

00:18:09.141 --> 00:18:10.819
I thought it was interesting.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:15.160
While we can see that social
media data are found data,

00:18:15.161 --> 00:18:17.065
research data are crafted data.

00:18:17.066 --> 00:18:19.625
So this difference between
crafted and found.

00:18:19.626 --> 00:18:23.600
Crafted because, again,
research data are

00:18:23.601 --> 00:18:27.585
collected to ask
a specific hypothesis,

00:18:27.586 --> 00:18:29.499
also large scale datasets,

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:30.960
at least in my community,

00:18:30.961 --> 00:18:34.095
and with the researchers
that I work with,

00:18:34.096 --> 00:18:35.460
not maybe all research data,

00:18:35.461 --> 00:18:39.915
but I believe there is
always a hypothesis in mind.

00:18:39.916 --> 00:18:44.590
And, so, what I was saying?

00:18:44.591 --> 00:18:47.885
And then within a specific
experimental setting,

00:18:47.886 --> 00:18:50.685
and to ask specific
research questions.

00:18:50.686 --> 00:18:54.000
Okay. The second thing
that I want to

00:18:54.001 --> 00:18:57.270
introduce to you is the
open data movement in science,

00:18:57.271 --> 00:18:58.620
which is different from

00:18:58.621 --> 00:19:00.900
the open data movement
in government.

00:19:00.901 --> 00:19:04.960
For example, biology and

00:19:04.961 --> 00:19:09.565
biomedicine has a long history
of data sharing and reuse.

00:19:09.566 --> 00:19:11.320
So biology has been
doing this for

00:19:11.321 --> 00:19:13.750
a long time, and then, again,

00:19:13.751 --> 00:19:16.450
I read a lot of books about it,

00:19:16.451 --> 00:19:19.240
but which help me to really to

00:19:19.241 --> 00:19:22.000
characterize the new
open data movement.

00:19:22.001 --> 00:19:24.370
So how is it now different?

00:19:24.371 --> 00:19:28.590
Two main reasons: first of all,

00:19:28.591 --> 00:19:31.150
now making data
publicly available is

00:19:31.151 --> 00:19:34.090
becoming a requirement
by funding agencies.

00:19:34.091 --> 00:19:36.835
So if you want to
collect new datasets,

00:19:36.836 --> 00:19:39.725
you have to- again,

00:19:39.726 --> 00:19:40.860
you will see that this is

00:19:40.861 --> 00:19:44.175
specifically what is going
on in my case study,

00:19:44.176 --> 00:19:46.920
but if you want to
collect the data,

00:19:46.921 --> 00:19:50.010
you have to basically

00:19:50.011 --> 00:19:52.350
demonstrate that you will make

00:19:52.351 --> 00:19:54.030
the data publicly available.

00:19:54.031 --> 00:19:56.450
While, before, traditionally,

00:19:56.451 --> 00:19:59.440
the way a biologist shared
the data is like this,

00:19:59.441 --> 00:20:00.820
'Okay, I am a biologist,

00:20:00.821 --> 00:20:03.040
I collect the dataset and then

00:20:03.041 --> 00:20:05.820
I mine and analyze my dataset,

00:20:05.821 --> 00:20:08.200
I publish a publication,

00:20:08.201 --> 00:20:12.700
then along with the publication
I release my raw data or

00:20:12.701 --> 00:20:16.350
some summary-level data along

00:20:16.351 --> 00:20:19.680
with my publication
and then a biocurator,

00:20:19.681 --> 00:20:23.805
who is this figure in
biology and databases,

00:20:23.806 --> 00:20:27.570
comes in and harvests the data
from the papers and from

00:20:27.571 --> 00:20:29.760
the open repositories and

00:20:29.761 --> 00:20:32.400
then makes available the
data to the public.'

00:20:32.401 --> 00:20:35.550
So usually, traditionally,
data are released

00:20:35.551 --> 00:20:39.960
after publication in
the traditional open movement.

00:20:39.961 --> 00:20:41.130
And it's also very much

00:20:41.131 --> 00:20:42.620
community-driven. Please
ask your question.

00:20:42.621 --> 00:20:44.850
&gt;&gt; Just for clarification,
is the biocurator a human

00:20:44.851 --> 00:20:47.415
being or is it a machine?

00:20:47.416 --> 00:20:50.595
&gt;&gt; So, for now,
it's a human being.

00:20:50.596 --> 00:20:53.340
We will see. But
now it's actually,

00:20:53.341 --> 00:20:55.650
I believe it has to
be. You never know.

00:20:55.651 --> 00:20:59.910
But a biocurator is simply
a very important role,

00:20:59.911 --> 00:21:02.115
because they usually
are people that know

00:21:02.116 --> 00:21:05.040
biology ontology really well.

00:21:05.041 --> 00:21:06.990
So they have this
idea of how knowledge

00:21:06.991 --> 00:21:09.905
is represented and organized.

00:21:09.906 --> 00:21:12.300
So they harvest
the summary-level data

00:21:12.301 --> 00:21:14.730
from publications and
then they curate it at

00:21:14.731 --> 00:21:17.610
the metadata and onto
logical terms and

00:21:17.611 --> 00:21:19.755
relationships and then they

00:21:19.756 --> 00:21:21.930
upload it on bioinformatics
tools. Please.

00:21:21.931 --> 00:21:23.040
&gt;&gt; I'm just trying to say if they

00:21:23.041 --> 00:21:25.020
attached to a specific journal
or if [inaudible].

00:21:25.021 --> 00:21:29.640
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:21:29.641 --> 00:21:31.620
so in my experience, and I think,

00:21:31.621 --> 00:21:34.470
probably most of
the cases, biocurators,

00:21:34.471 --> 00:21:39.280
they work for different
platforms so databases,

00:21:39.281 --> 00:21:40.930
they have their own biocurators.

00:21:40.931 --> 00:21:45.010
So, maintain the platform
and also harvest

00:21:45.011 --> 00:21:50.445
data from different
publications and journals.

00:21:50.446 --> 00:21:54.250
So, it used to be more
community-driven,

00:21:54.251 --> 00:21:56.340
open data in genomic medicine.

00:21:56.341 --> 00:21:58.814
Now it's more like, it's
increasingly required,

00:21:58.815 --> 00:22:00.240
still a lot of scientists

00:22:00.241 --> 00:22:01.710
that do not to share their data,

00:22:01.711 --> 00:22:04.340
but what I'm talking
about are the new grants

00:22:04.341 --> 00:22:07.820
have been accepted. Please.

00:22:07.821 --> 00:22:10.565
&gt;&gt; If new grants are approved,
who is requiring this?

00:22:10.566 --> 00:22:12.785
&gt;&gt; Right. Funding bodies.

00:22:12.786 --> 00:22:15.575
Funding bodies like
funding agency.

00:22:15.576 --> 00:22:16.550
&gt;&gt; NIH or like?

00:22:16.551 --> 00:22:19.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah. Basically, NIH.

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:23.415
Because I've worked
with biomedical data,

00:22:23.416 --> 00:22:26.399
so my case study is NIH-funded,

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:29.965
a group of collaboration.

00:22:29.966 --> 00:22:32.835
Yes. But I know that also

00:22:32.836 --> 00:22:36.015
some private foundations are
increasing requiring it,

00:22:36.016 --> 00:22:37.470
but they mainly work with

00:22:37.471 --> 00:22:42.850
publicly funded data
collections. So, yeah.

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:46.230
&gt;&gt; Is this only on genomics
or there's another?

00:22:46.231 --> 00:22:49.190
&gt;&gt; Let me think.

00:22:49.191 --> 00:22:50.825
I think it's that

00:22:50.826 --> 00:22:53.060
biomedicine is like 90 percent

00:22:53.061 --> 00:22:54.770
genomic right now, I think.

00:22:54.771 --> 00:22:58.660
So, yeah.

00:22:58.661 --> 00:23:00.120
I would say there is

00:23:00.121 --> 00:23:03.060
a specifically genomic
data for sure.

00:23:03.061 --> 00:23:10.770
We'll see some few
more about it but-

00:23:10.771 --> 00:23:11.770
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:23:10.772 --> 00:23:14.415
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes. All data
needs to be identified.

00:23:14.416 --> 00:23:15.900
My work is not that much

00:23:15.901 --> 00:23:19.020
about privacy than
identifications,

00:23:19.021 --> 00:23:20.820
but there is a lot
of work in that.

00:23:20.821 --> 00:23:21.990
They're showing that, yes,

00:23:21.991 --> 00:23:23.955
it identified, but
to some extent,

00:23:23.956 --> 00:23:28.195
you can't read and
de-identify a DNA or someone,

00:23:28.196 --> 00:23:30.510
so there is a lot of
research in that direction.

00:23:30.511 --> 00:23:33.390
But for sure, these data
are shared with

00:23:33.391 --> 00:23:37.320
specific de-identification
techniques even though,

00:23:37.321 --> 00:23:39.395
as you will see, my community

00:23:39.396 --> 00:23:42.985
collects face images
from children,

00:23:42.986 --> 00:23:46.080
so that's impossible to
de-identify, obviously.

00:23:46.081 --> 00:23:48.450
I mean, if that's
how do you do it.

00:23:48.451 --> 00:23:52.200
So, but yes, there are
a lot of requirement,

00:23:52.201 --> 00:23:54.910
they're very careful
with privacy.

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:57.560
Okay. So now the idea is

00:23:57.561 --> 00:24:01.225
that data are
required to be sure,

00:24:01.226 --> 00:24:02.740
but also there is

00:24:02.741 --> 00:24:04.900
this portion making
the data available right

00:24:04.901 --> 00:24:08.810
after data collection
before publication,

00:24:08.811 --> 00:24:10.560
before the scientist, before

00:24:10.561 --> 00:24:12.610
the data creator herself

00:24:12.611 --> 00:24:14.965
can mind and make
sense of the data.

00:24:14.966 --> 00:24:17.560
The idea is that when the data

00:24:17.561 --> 00:24:19.935
comes back up from
the sequencing facility,

00:24:19.936 --> 00:24:21.530
you have to make it available,

00:24:21.531 --> 00:24:23.700
or usually, give you six months,

00:24:23.701 --> 00:24:25.420
which is really not that much to

00:24:25.421 --> 00:24:29.830
analyze a larger scale data
set of genomic data.

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:35.705
So why all this interest in
making this data available,

00:24:35.706 --> 00:24:37.480
requiring to make data available

00:24:37.481 --> 00:24:39.595
right after data collection?

00:24:39.596 --> 00:24:42.040
So, I read a lot of

00:24:42.041 --> 00:24:44.740
documents and grants
under different reasons

00:24:44.741 --> 00:24:47.500
why different people wants

00:24:47.501 --> 00:24:50.335
to push for these
open data movement.

00:24:50.336 --> 00:24:54.310
But I think from the funding
bodies point of view,

00:24:54.311 --> 00:24:57.250
there is this idea of
a fast efficient science.

00:24:57.251 --> 00:25:00.445
We want to make research,
especially public research,

00:25:00.446 --> 00:25:04.310
as fast and as
efficient as possible.

00:25:04.311 --> 00:25:07.540
So if you have one
large scale data set,

00:25:07.541 --> 00:25:09.470
and then the person collected it,

00:25:09.471 --> 00:25:11.675
and the only person
who is minding it

00:25:11.676 --> 00:25:14.090
to ask my own research question,

00:25:14.091 --> 00:25:15.725
they're not subtlety efficient.

00:25:15.726 --> 00:25:17.630
In an ideal situation,

00:25:17.631 --> 00:25:19.535
you want to have

00:25:19.536 --> 00:25:22.565
10 other researchers
who at the same time,

00:25:22.566 --> 00:25:25.310
while I am analyzing
my own data set,

00:25:25.311 --> 00:25:28.895
they come in and they ask
different research questions,

00:25:28.896 --> 00:25:30.470
each of them, right?

00:25:30.471 --> 00:25:32.375
They're different also from mine

00:25:32.376 --> 00:25:34.640
or from the same data set,

00:25:34.641 --> 00:25:39.275
and this is the big promise
of open data in biomedicine.

00:25:39.276 --> 00:25:42.485
The idea that you can do that
in a way that can be like,

00:25:42.486 --> 00:25:45.390
so the research process
is very efficient and you

00:25:45.391 --> 00:25:48.780
have a high return on
investment, right?

00:25:48.781 --> 00:25:51.980
So, I believe that from
the funder's point of view,

00:25:51.981 --> 00:25:54.365
is mainly a return
of investment idea.

00:25:54.366 --> 00:25:56.740
But in order to do that,

00:25:56.741 --> 00:25:59.295
you have to make data fungible,

00:25:59.296 --> 00:26:02.010
which is not my expression.

00:26:02.011 --> 00:26:06.140
Actually, Sabina Leonelli,
in her brand new book,

00:26:06.141 --> 00:26:09.210
Data-Driven Biology,
she talks about

00:26:09.211 --> 00:26:13.065
this move of making
biology data fungible data.

00:26:13.066 --> 00:26:14.865
And if you know
someone from Econ,

00:26:14.866 --> 00:26:18.000
by you probably recognized
that these are economics term,

00:26:18.001 --> 00:26:20.990
which basically means that you

00:26:20.991 --> 00:26:23.630
have to have these data turn

00:26:23.631 --> 00:26:25.700
into some commodities that

00:26:25.701 --> 00:26:29.180
are interchangeable and that
they speak for themselves.

00:26:29.181 --> 00:26:33.185
So you have to make this in
a way that anyone can come,

00:26:33.186 --> 00:26:36.590
and trust this data and
mind this data even though

00:26:36.591 --> 00:26:38.120
this researcher did not

00:26:38.121 --> 00:26:42.330
witness the collection of
the data in the first place.

00:26:43.060 --> 00:26:45.260
So how do you do it?

00:26:45.261 --> 00:26:47.975
You either try to make
them context-free,

00:26:47.976 --> 00:26:50.165
or as Sabina Leonelli seen,

00:26:50.166 --> 00:26:51.470
you have to pack it,

00:26:51.471 --> 00:26:54.930
the data set, in a way
that can travel and

00:26:54.931 --> 00:26:56.430
can go into this journey

00:26:56.431 --> 00:26:58.440
between different
research situations.

00:26:58.441 --> 00:27:01.970
So this is idea of data packages.

00:27:01.971 --> 00:27:05.210
Okay, I think I've
said enough about it.

00:27:05.211 --> 00:27:07.880
Okay. So these are my main three

00:27:07.881 --> 00:27:15.230
research questions, basically.

00:27:15.231 --> 00:27:17.510
When I was reviewing
all these literature,

00:27:17.511 --> 00:27:20.000
and I got very much
interested into figuring

00:27:20.001 --> 00:27:21.230
